justified.evidence_quote:
The strongest defense is that:

- The recommendation was only 15% of the portfolio;
- The fund was diversified and professionally managed;
- The duration was moderate;
- The Chens had an income objective and wanted higher monthly income;
- The rest of the portfolio remained conservative;
- The loss was modest;
- The recommendation was documented and compliance-approved.

The likely regulatory conclusion would be:

- No strong finding of fraud or intentional churning;
- Possible finding that the trading strategy was not fully in the customer’s best interest because of repeated avoidable redemption fees;
- Possible criticism or sanction for inadequate cost analysis, mutual fund switching, and use of high-fee products for short-term trading;
- Possible requirement for restitution of some or all redemption fees and transaction charges, especially the $14,200 in short-term redemption penalties;
